          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"shakespeare-king-lear-97d3418f54f0370b\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Cordelia directly avows that she loves Lear, making truth-conditional force dominant. “According to my bond” also invokes the filial obligation structuring that love, giving substantial performative support, though she affirms an existing bond rather than undertaking a new commitment. Her controlled refusal to turn her heart into theatrical speech provides no independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is the strength of P: “according to my bond” invokes filial obligation, but the utterance primarily describes the love and duty Cordelia already bears rather than creating or renewing them.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Cordelia directly addresses Lear during his public love test.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Lear demands a public declaration that will determine Cordelia’s inheritance. Cordelia rejects her sisters’ expansive rhetoric and offers a deliberately bounded avowal: she loves Lear truly, but only in the measure proper to a daughter’s bond, leaving room for the future claims of a husband. The coercive and transactional setting explains the speech’s restraint without changing its core T/P force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love your majesty / According to my bond; no more nor less.”\",\"supports\":\"A direct avowal of love strongly supports T, while the explicit appeal to a filial bond independently supports P.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I cannot heave / My heart into my mouth”\",\"supports\":\"Cordelia explicitly resists converting inward feeling into ostentatious verbal display, weighing against expressive/reflexive E.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Cordelia explains: “I / Return those duties back as are right fit, / Obey you, love you, and most honour you.”\",\"supports\":\"Her elaboration presents love as both a genuine state and part of an established reciprocal filial duty.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Cordelia says her future husband will carry “Half my love with him, half my care and duty.”\",\"supports\":\"She carefully limits the scope of the filial bond rather than making an unlimited relational undertaking to Lear.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Cordelia affirms that her heart goes with the statement and characterizes herself as “true.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding exchange frames the utterance as a sincere avowal rather than empty rhetoric or instrumental language.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally capture the utterance as a truthful avowal articulated through an existing filial obligation. The coercive love test, rhetorical restraint, and contrast with Cordelia’s sisters are contextual features rather than missing dimensions of core force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
